> Login to the Github
> Go to the Project Repository
> Click on the "Fork" option
> get the repository url
> Now go to the local machine and clone the repository (git clone url)
> Use (git log --oneline) to check all history
> Use (git remote -v) to check fetch and push HTTPS urls
> Go to the original project repository in Github
> Copy the original HTTPS url
> Now add the original HTTPS url in local (git remote add upstream url)
> Check the fetch and push url (git remote -v)
> Add some change in original and modify file in original repository (upstream)
> Go to the "Fork" copy and Github display this branch is behind "Upstream"
  repository
> To get latest changes from "Upstream" in local (git pull upstream main)
> Check (git log)
> Perform change in local machine
> Perform (git status)
> Perform (git add <file-name>)
> Perform the commit (git commit -m "add commit")
> Perform push to the fork (git push origin <branch-name>)
> Go to the Github of "Fork" and it display "This branch is 1 commit ahead of
  "Upstream"
> Click on the "Pull Request"
> Click on the "Create Pull Request" button
> Then add proper message and "Create Pull Request" button
> Go to the original project repository
> Open the "Pull Request" section in Github
> Open the PR (Pull Request)
> Review and confirm pull request so that fork changes will apply to the original
  project repository